why 1 1/2 CROSSING The tenor walks down G–F♯–E while the bass steps up to A before dropping an octave. For a beat the bass is above the tenor; the octave leap puts it back below.
why 1 5/8 CROSSING The same beat: the tenor reaches E under the bass's A. On the next beat the bass drops an octave.
why 1 11/16 CROSSING The last sixteenth of the crossing, while the alto passes C♯–B. On beat 4 the bass is far below again.
why 6 0 LEADING_TONE The tenor's G♯, leading tone of A, leaps up to C♯, the third of the A chord at the fermata. Rising to A would leave the chord without its third.
why 10 0 LEADING_TONE A deceptive cadence: V⁷ moves to vi instead of I. The alto already takes D, so the tenor's C♯ falls to B and doubles the root of B minor.
why 11 1/4 SIMILAR_MOTION Everything rises toward the cadence on A: the bass's G♯ is the leading tone of A and the soprano climbs B–C♯–D. Each line has its own reason to rise.
